<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="44" d:DesignHeight="44"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Controls.KeyCapView"
             x:DataType="vm:KeyboardKeyViewModel"
             ClipToBounds="False">
  <!-- One key cap. The cap is a Button so it is focusable and clickable without code-behind; all
       of its chrome comes from the KeyCapButton control theme in Themes/ControlThemes/Keycap.axaml,
       which owns the template, the face ladder (selected / listening / locked) and the four state
       badges (remap bar, macro dot, tap-and-hold corner, advisory bar). The click goes to the
       editor through the containing KeyboardView, so nothing here knows what a remap is
       (specs/10-apps-and-ui.md, "Remap workflow").

       The classes below say what the KEY is; `stateBadges` says whether this SURFACE draws it, and
       comes from ShowsStateBadge for the same reason the LED strip's row comes from ShowsLedStrip
       — see the property's own summary. Both are read off this control rather than off the data.

       ClipToBounds is off on the way out as well as in: a focused cap casts a 3px halo past its own
       bounds into a grid whose caps are 4px apart, and a clip anywhere along the chain erases it. -->
  <Button Classes="keyCap"
          ClipToBounds="False"
          Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}"
          Classes.listening="{Binding IsListening}"
          Classes.modified="{Binding IsModified}"
          Classes.macro="{Binding IsMacro}"
          Classes.tapHold="{Binding IsTapAndHold}"
          Classes.advisory="{Binding HasAdvisory}"
          Classes.locked="{Binding !CanEdit}"
          Classes.stateBadges="{Binding $parent[controls:KeyCapView].ShowsStateBadge}"
          Command="{Binding $parent[controls:KeyboardView].KeySelectedCommand}"
          CommandParameter="{Binding}"
          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Caption}">
    <!--
      THE CAP'S VERTICAL BUDGET, which is the whole reason this grid is shaped the way it is.

      A 1U cap is 30x26 at mock scale (KeycapPitchY 30 − KeycapGap 4), and the KeyCapButton theme
      draws it with a 1px border and 2px of padding. That padding exists FOR THE BADGES — it is what
      puts them the mockups' 2px inside the border — so it is not reduced; the grid steps back over
      it instead with `Margin="0,-2"`, exactly as the caption below steps over it horizontally with
      its own `Margin="-2,0"`. Nothing in the template moves, and the content box is the cap's whole
      inner area: 28 wide x 24 tall.

      Into those 24 go, at their natural (uncut) metrics — IBM Plex Sans is 1.3em tall, so a 9px
      line is 11.7 and a 7px line is 9.1, and Avalonia rounds a desired size up to the DIP:

          caption, one 9px line ......................... 12
          caption, two 8px lines (stacked) .............. 21
          silkscreen sub-legend, 7px .................... 10  − 2px of shared leading
          LED strip ......................................  3  + 0px gap

      which leaves the three boards this cap is ever drawn on:

          Layout board .................. 12                 of 24   (12 spare)
          Layout board, sub-legend ...... 12 − 2 + 10 = 20   of 24   ( 4 spare)
          Layout board, stacked ......... 21                 of 24   ( 3 spare)
          Lighting board ................ 12 + 3      = 15   of 24   ( 9 spare)
          Lighting board, stacked ....... 21 + 3      = 24   of 24   ( 0 spare)

      Two of those five needed a decision rather than an arrangement, and both are taken here: the
      sub-legend is NOT DRAWN on a lighting board (it and the strip want the same band), and a
      caption carrying its own line break steps down to `FontSizeKeycapLabelStacked`. The last row
      is exact rather than comfortable, and the suite holds it: KeycapThemeTests asserts that no
      legend on any of the three boards is arranged shorter than the text it holds actually needs.
    -->
    <Grid RowDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto" Margin="0,-2">
      <!-- A '\n' in the caption is a two-line cap (specs/05-key-model.md §1.1); wrapping is on so
           long captions on a 1U cap break instead of being clipped mid-word. `stacked` is that same
           fact as a class — see Styles/Keyboard.axaml for the step it takes and why.

           `Margin="-2,0"` gives the caption back the 2px the theme pads the cap with, horizontally;
           the grid's own `Margin="0,-2"` above does the other axis. At mock scale a 1U cap is 30
           wide, so the padded box leaves 24px for a legend. "Pause" is a 1U key and sets exactly
           24px at the 9/400 step, so it wrapped and the cap read "Paus". The badges keep their
           inset; only the text spans the 28.

           It stretches and centres its TEXT rather than centring the block, and that is the half
           that actually fixed it: a non-stretched child is arranged at `min(available, desired)`,
           so a caption that measures 24 is arranged at 24 however much room the negative margin
           won it — and the wrap comes back on the fraction of a pixel. Stretch hands the line the
           whole 28, and `TextAlignment` does the centring the alignment used to. -->
      <TextBlock Grid.Row="0"
                 Classes="keyCapText"
                 Classes.locked="{Binding !CanEdit}"
                 Classes.stacked="{Binding IsCaptionStacked}"
                 Margin="-2,0"
                 Text="{Binding Caption}"
                 TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 TextAlignment="Center"
                 HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />

      <!-- THE SECONDARY LEGEND — the shifted character or the device hotkey the board PRINTS under
           the main one ('!', 'mute', 'scr lk'). It is silkscreen, so it never changes with a remap
           and never notifies; a position whose print says nothing the caption does not carries
           none, and the row then collapses to nothing so an ordinary cap keeps its legend centred.

           UNDER THE CAPTION, CENTRED, which is how mockup 1e stacks the pair (`F1` over `mute`, a
           column with `align-items: center`). The assignment leads and the silkscreen follows it:
           what the key does now is the thing being read, and what the board printed on it is the
           context. It used to be drawn above the caption, which inverted that.

           NOT DRAWN AT ALL ON A LIGHTING BOARD. The strip below wants this same band, a 26px cap
           has one band to give, and a lighting board is about colour — the same call, for the same
           reason, that already hides the four state badges there (KeyCapView.ShowsStateBadge). The
           `Panel` is what carries that half of the answer: an invisible panel takes no row, and the
           legend's own emptiness check stays a plain binding rather than a converter over two. -->
      <Panel Grid.Row="1" IsVisible="{Binding !$parent[controls:KeyCapView].ShowsLedStrip}">
        <TextBlock Classes="keyCapSubText"
                   Text="{Binding SecondaryLegend}"
                   IsVisible="{Binding SecondaryLegend, Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                   VerticalAlignment="Top" />
      </Panel>

      <!-- The key's LED colour (ledN.txt), shown as a strip rather than as the cap background so
           the cap's own state stays readable next to it. Three states, and they need two answers
           from two different places:

             1. LIGHTING IS NOT ON SCREEN — no strip at all. The Keys tab edits assignments; a row
                of LED bars under every cap on it is a readout of something nobody asked about, and
                the mockups draw no such row (docs/design/mockups.md, 1e and 2a).
             2. LIGHTING IS ON SCREEN AND THIS LED IS OFF — the hatch. "Off is hatched, never black"
                (docs/design/handoff.md:105 and :151, and again at mockups.md:176 and :333), because
                black is a colour a key can legitimately be lit.
             3. LIGHTING IS ON SCREEN AND THIS LED IS LIT — the colour, covering the hatch.

           HasColorOverlay is the second answer and means exactly "this key is lit". The first is
           ShowsLedStrip, and it is a property of the PICTURE rather than of the key: the Keys tab
           and the Lighting tab render the same KeyboardKeyViewModel instances (see
           KeyboardLayerViewModel.ApplyColorOverlays), so no state on the cap's DataContext could
           ever separate them. KeyboardView owns it and hands it to each cap it builds.

           The cap does not change size either way — KeyboardPanel lays every cap out from its unit
           rectangle — but the caption's box inside it is 3px shorter on a lighting board, which is
           the row this strip occupies.

           NO TOP MARGIN, unlike the sub-legend above it: those 3px are the last three of the cap's
           24 (see the budget at the top of this file), and a stacked caption needs the other 21
           exactly. It reads as a gap anyway, because the caption's box ends below its last line's
           ink by the font's own descent — about 1px at the stacked step, 2.5 at the full one — and
           a one-line caption is centred in the 21 that are left, which puts 4px of air over it. -->
      <Border Grid.Row="2"
              Height="3"
              CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusChip}"
              Margin="1,0"
              ClipToBounds="True"
              IsVisible="{Binding $parent[controls:KeyCapView].ShowsLedStrip}"
              Background="{DynamicResource HatchBrush}">
        <Border CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusChip}"
                IsVisible="{Binding HasColorOverlay}"
                Background="{Binding ColorOverlayHex, Converter={StaticResource HexColorToBrush}}" />
      </Border>
    </Grid>
  </Button>
</UserControl>
